# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# The lexer refuses a source file containing a carriage return, on purpose
# and with a good message: "This file uses Windows line endings.
# ZDeceptron files end a line with `\n` alone." (`zdc-lexer/src/layout.rs`.)
#
# Git on Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites LF to
# CRLF *on checkout*. So a Windows clone of this repository got a working
# `zdc` and a tree of `.zd` files that same `zdc` would not compile —
# including every example. Found by the first run of the release workflow:
# the Windows job built the binary, printed `zdc 0.1.0`, and then failed
# its own smoke test on `examples/hello.zd`.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides that conversion for the files it names, so a checkout
# is byte-identical on every platform.
#
# This fixes the repository. It does not fix a Windows user writing their
# own `.zd` in an editor that saves CRLF — that is a language question
# rather than a repository one, and it is filed separately.

# Everything the compiler reads, and everything that is compared byte for
# byte in a test.
*.zd    text eol=lf
*.rs    text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF whatever the platform: a CRLF shebang line
# makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage
# return, and the error it gives says the file does not exist.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Not text, and must never be line-ending converted.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
